WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.620
Politicians should be on social media

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:05.750
because it's also their way
of connecting with people,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.840
and they certainly have
a right to free speech.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.850
So, I don't think it was easy for Meta

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.430
to decide whether Mr. Trump
should be taken down or not.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.750
And we actually also touched
upon in our decision,

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.810
that Trump should not
be taken down because

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.360
he was saying falsehoods
about the election.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.780
We only said that it was
right to take Mr. Trump down

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.093
because he was inciting violence.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.260
Hello and welcome,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.500
billions of posts, from billions of users,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.890
political, social, and financial power

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.800
bigger than some nation states.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.360
So, how do you oversee Facebook?

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.050
That job falls to the relatively new

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.720
Facebook Oversight Board
dubbed the social network's

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.530
so-called Supreme Court.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.040
And my guest today is its
co-chair, Helle Thorning-Schmidt,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.590
who was Denmark's first
female Prime Minister.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.140
She led the center-left
Social Democrats Party

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.480
in a coalition and held that
post for four years until 2016.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.390
Now, she's helping to
oversee the tech titan,

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.940
which operates under
the parent company Meta,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.560
and also owns WhatsApp and Instagram.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.950
So, can it really be held to account,

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.680
even by former world leaders,
professors, charity bosses,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.060
and Nobel laureates?

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.010
At a time when the social network business

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.800
can block a U.S. president,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.740
and as it tries to curb
COVID misinformation,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.880
arguably, it's a more
important role than ever.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.252
Helle Thorning-Schmidt, welcome.

00:01:38.252 --> 00:01:39.930
Thank you very much.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.790
Well, we'll get to that role shortly,

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.200
but I wanted to just come to something

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.410
which I find fascinating about you.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:46.700
You were 48,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.780
when you left the
biggest job of your life.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.130
It's actually very interesting

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.790
because I think that us, prime ministers,

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.660
we've become younger and younger.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.050
And that also means that we leave earlier.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.590
Many former prime ministers,

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.150
they actually yeah, died on the job.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.420
But we are a generation of politicians

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.930
that came into politics quite early,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.920
and are also leaving quite early.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.510
And that means that I was just before 50,

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.770
and had just to shake my head a bit

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.230
and find out what's there for me

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.370
for the rest of my working life.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.750
And was extremely fortunate
that I got in contact

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.430
with Save the Children
International here in London,

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:25.810
and started talking to them

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.290
because they needed a new CEO.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.050
And I took over that role,

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.000
and was so happy to start a new chapter

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.900
with something so meaningful
as working for children

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:35.740
all over the world.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.970
Can you remember that the day after

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.480
you ended being Prime Minister?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.790
I mean, did you just get
your jogging bottoms on,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.180
your slippers, did you slob about?

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.013
What do you do?

00:02:44.013 --> 00:02:46.370
It is so weird to stop
being Prime Minister.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.944
And I think there's nothing
that compares to that.

00:02:48.944 --> 00:02:51.400
You just, your whole life changes.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.430
And I remember very clearly coming in,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.950
hugging my chauffeurs and my,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.380
the police that had been
helping me all the way through,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.060
hug them goodbye outside my house,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.823
walked in, closed the door behind me,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.180
and quiet, just quiet.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.380
No one phones you,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.080
the office, the party, you're
used to people connecting

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.590
and talking to you all the time,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.150
and stuff happening all the time.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.630
And it was just quiet.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.060
So, I remember that quiet.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.300
It was a tristesse, not like a depression,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.610
but a little bit of a situation where

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.370
you're not quite sure
how you live in this life

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.230
without all the buzz that was around you.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.855
But I watched Game of Thrones for a month.

00:03:35.855 --> 00:03:37.200
I got a...
For a month?

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.033
No, not really.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.740
I got our bikes repaired so I
could bike round Copenhagen.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.650
There are so many things
I hadn't done on my bike,

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.310
or just walking around Copenhagen.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.760
That was my way of transitioning

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.640
into just being a normal person again.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.100
So, you mentioned about
becoming the Chief Executive

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.720
of Save the Children
International, that came to an end.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:54.930
And your next role,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.060
which is as I was just
saying in my introduction,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.520
it's such an important and pivotal moment

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.430
to have such a role,

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.450
is co-chair of this Facebook-

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.120
Yeah.
Oversight Board.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.060
I just wanted to ask, first of
all, who asked you to do it?

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.030
And why did you want to do it?

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.089
Well, we all know that
social media has come up,

00:04:12.089 --> 00:04:16.670
and has for a long, long
time been very unregulated.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:21.100
And basically, what we saw was
that the ultimate decisions

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.760
about what content stays
up on social media,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:26.070
or gets removed,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.600
is taken by the social media
company companies themselves,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.800
and ultimately, Mark Zuckerberg.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.850
And somehow that doesn't feel right.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:38.270
We need probably more
regulation on, of social media,

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.850
but it also seemed a
better way of doing it.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.540
That Facebook was actually outsourcing

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.323
to an independent body,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.360
how their content should be reviewed.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.590
And so, basically the reason
why I took on the role

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.850
is because I consider the
Oversight Board independent.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.240
Facebook has to listen to our decisions,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.090
they have to follow our decisions.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:04.110
And we also have this
opportunity to give them advice,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:07.520
or recommendations of what
they should do on social media.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.100
And it goes both for
Facebook, and for Instagram.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.160
So, unless we have been
promised complete independence

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.820
and that they would actually
abide by our decisions

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.840
and follow our decisions,
I would not have done it.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.780
So, it was basically Facebook's leadership

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.066
asking me to do this.

00:05:23.066 --> 00:05:26.090
And we look very, very
carefully into whether

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.050
this would be an
institution that would be,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.370
have that complete independence.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.566
We'll lets definitely come to the

00:05:30.566 --> 00:05:32.440
and test the independence
in just a moment if we can,

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.040
but are you comfortable
with the amount of power

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.400
Meta has overseeing Facebook,
Instagram, WhatsApp?

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.450
Yes and no.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:43.010
I mean, I think social media
has been too unregulated

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:44.650
for too many years,

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.670
and obviously the Oversight Board

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.300
is not dealing with everything Meta.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.350
There'll be tech discussions
and other discussions,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:54.200
anti-trust discussions that
we are not dealing with,

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.770
we're dealing with content.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.530
But there is absolutely no doubt

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.450
that until we started our work,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.320
Meta was left too alone,

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.930
in terms of deciding on content,

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.250
whether content should
stay up or get removed,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.880
making their own rules as they went along,

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:12.720
the transparency was missing.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.520
It still is, and that is
why we need regulation.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:16.730
And we need an oversight board.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.070
What do you say to
those who are concerned,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:22.450
that you are funded by
Facebook, to police Facebook?

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:27.430
Well, Facebook cannot interfere
in any of our decisions.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.570
They have to live with our decisions.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.670
When we decided on the case with,

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:33.550
regarding Trump, for example,

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.020
they had to follow our decisions.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.110
And I don't talk to,

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.450
none of us talk to Facebook

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.890
about how we should decide anything.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.150
So, it's up to each
and everyone to decide,

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:46.000
do they think that's independence?

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.229
I don't take any orders from Facebook.

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:49.100
You don't take any orders,

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.778
but you can't necessarily know
what they're not telling you.

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:53.611
No.

00:06:53.611 --> 00:06:54.920
And for the world, you know,

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.610
the whistleblower Frances-
Yeah.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.750
Haugen, came to people's attention.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:06:59.583
Yeah.

00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:00.416
She came and spoke aloud.

00:07:00.416 --> 00:07:02.900
For instance, about that
high profile Facebook users,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:06.660
got to follow different rules
and receive special treatment.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.570
I mean, how could you
trust Facebook after that?

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.857
You have to trust Facebook to tell you.

00:07:10.857 --> 00:07:13.950
No, we have to find a re-trust
in Facebook after that.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.880
Because for example, on the crosschecks

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.200
that Haugen was talking about,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.680
there's no doubt that we
didn't know enough about that,

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:24.660
which we have criticized
Meta for not telling us,

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.570
sufficiently clear
about these crosschecks.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:28.930
Also when we asked about it,

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.150
we didn't get sufficient answers.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.560
So, this is something we
are going to follow up on.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.430
And I think, just the fact we have existed

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.780
for a little more than a year now,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.210
has shed some light on
the lack of transparency.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.440
The crosschecks, the fact
that they don't talk about

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:45.630
these things in public,

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:50.540
and other aspects of the lack
of transparency with Meta.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:51.564
So, I do think that...

00:07:51.564 --> 00:07:52.540
Where you were annoyed when you saw

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:53.950
what Frances had to say?

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.350
I was, I, yeah, some of it, yes.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.510
I was annoyed, when I found
out that the crosschecks

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:01.680
that we had been asking Meta about,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.308
they had not given fulfilling
answer to our questions.

00:08:06.308 --> 00:08:07.770
What do you mean
crosschecks, sorry, when you?

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.070
Crosschecks is what they do with these,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.570
high profile individuals,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:13.960
Right.
That has an account.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.880
Where there seem to be special rules

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:18.300
for high profile individuals.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.310
And you hadn't been told about that?

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:20.610
We had not been told about it.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.270
And when we asked in the
context of the Trump case,

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.240
that we looked at,
Yes.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.740
They didn't give us a fulfilling answer,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.020
which we have criticized also publicly.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.610
So this, I think actually
underlines that you're right.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.000
We don't know what we don't know.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.270
But gradually, when Meta is
forced to open up more to us

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:39.310
and then also...

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:40.780
But by a whistle-blower?

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.220
Yeah, but that's absolutely,

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.660
but that's why it's good
to have whistle-blowers.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:46.140
But I also think, our role here

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.890
is to keep asking those questions.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.190
We've also asked Frances
Haugen afterwards,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.260
we had interviews with her
because we invited her to talk...

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:53.093
So you have spoken to her.

00:08:53.093 --> 00:08:53.926
We have spoken to her yeah.

00:08:53.926 --> 00:08:56.160
And we're probably going
to speak to her again.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.270
So, we have spoken to her
and the Oversight Board

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.940
to get even more information.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:01.920
And we will continue to talk to anyone

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.750
who's got anything to say about Meta,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:05.660
that we think will help us in our work,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:08.227
and learn from that and
feed that back to Facebook.

00:09:08.227 --> 00:09:10.950
But knowing that you have been misled.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.060
I wouldn't call it misled.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.000
I think that's your word, not mine.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.210
Okay, so what word would you?

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.570
They didn't tell us the full picture.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:18.403
They didn't give us a full-

00:09:18.403 --> 00:09:19.236
Okay.
Picture of the crosschecks.

00:09:19.236 --> 00:09:21.070
Knowing that you've been left in the dark-

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.220
Yeah.
Over some things,

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.053
is that a better-

00:09:23.053 --> 00:09:23.886
Yeah.

00:09:23.886 --> 00:09:25.290
Way of putting it?
Yeah, it's details.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:28.410
How do you then trust Facebook?

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.730
Do you trust Facebook?

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.720
I think Facebook should
be much more transparent

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:34.110
with the Oversight Board,

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.030
but also with the general public,

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.250
which is actually more important.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.700
And I believe, that the
work that I'm part of,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.783
in the Oversight Board,
will have that effect.

00:09:42.783 --> 00:09:44.640
But, do you trust them?

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.960
You're the co-chair of the board.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.220
Yeah, I trust Facebook that
they give us the information

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.000
that we're asking for.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.370
Even though they haven't?

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.780
I don't think they were
completely aware that they didn't,

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:56.920
but that's a different question,

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.220
and we have criticized them for it.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.968
And there will be other
things in the future...

00:10:00.968 --> 00:10:03.560
Why did you think they
left you in the dark?

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.180
I don't know, you have to ask Meta that,

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:07.500
but I think the most
important, you asked me,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:08.990
do I trust Facebook?

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.210
I trust Facebook generally,

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.010
but that doesn't mean that
they will in this instance,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.230
but also further on down the line,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:21.230
there will be more areas
where we will criticize Meta.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:22.738
We criticize Meta,

00:10:22.738 --> 00:10:25.840
every time we take a decision on anything.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.910
We have already issued more than

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.690
80 recommendations for Meta.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.660
And we will keep doing that.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:35.250
We push them hard on some of these issues.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.880
So, I don't need to trust Meta completely.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:39.810
I need to trust that they
give us the information,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.460
and they answer our questions
truthfully, which I do.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.950
But I think that that will
actually result in Meta treating

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:49.350
their users better, and being
more transparent in general.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.270
Should Mark Zuckerberg still be in charge?

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:54.050
I think it would be very
wrong of me as a co-chair

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.800
of the Oversight Board to
start having opinions about

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:59.010
who leads Meta.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.250
So, I will refrain from
getting into that discussion.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.010
I will not do that,

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.090
because I think it will it'll mix up

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.470
what the role of the Oversight Board is-

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:09.303
Okay.

00:11:09.303 --> 00:11:10.136
Which is to look at the content.

00:11:10.136 --> 00:11:10.969
Fair enough, I get that.

00:11:10.969 --> 00:11:11.802
I just wondered if you-
Yeah.

00:11:11.802 --> 00:11:12.635
If you did have a view,

00:11:12.635 --> 00:11:13.560
I know you've got a lot of opinions-

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.585
Yes, I've got a lot of opinions.

00:11:15.585 --> 00:11:16.920
I'll tell you something people
do have a huge opinion about.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:17.870
You mentioned it briefly,

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.130
but I want to just get a little
bit more into the detail.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.940
Almost a year ago, to the day really,

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.850
Facebook decided to suspend-
Yeah.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.400
President Trump for sharing
a video of his support,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.500
to his supporters.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.680
Some of his supporters
were storming the Capitol.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.270
You upheld that view,

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.600
that decision to suspend him indefinitely,

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.710
but not permanently, is that right?

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:39.630
It was a very interesting situation.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.627
Where Facebook had decided to, now Meta,

00:11:42.627 --> 00:11:45.170
decide to remove Trump from the platforms,

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:48.140
based on the fact that
he was inciting violence.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.730
And they asked us whether
that was, the Oversight Board,

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.300
whether that was the right decision.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:53.940
That was one of, not one of the decisions

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.150
where user complaints,

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.670
it was actually Meta
asking the Oversight Board,

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.120
what we thought about this.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.671
And we agreed with Meta that they,

00:12:01.671 --> 00:12:05.360
it was okay to remove Mr. Trump

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.060
on the basis that he
was inciting violence,

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:11.610
but we disagreed with the
sanction that Meta had given.

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.120
So, we asked them actually to go back

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.820
and find a new sanction for Mr. Trump.

00:12:15.820 --> 00:12:19.210
So, it became more case about

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:23.420
Meta and an arbitrary sanction,
than it was about Mr. Trump.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:25.370
So it was sorting out the system,

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.090
as well as taking a view-

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:27.923
Yes.
On that.

00:12:27.923 --> 00:12:28.756
Yeah.
I understand that.

00:12:28.756 --> 00:12:33.220
But, some people did view that
as anti-democratic, you know,

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.000
75 million people voted for him.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:35.833
Yeah.

00:12:35.833 --> 00:12:38.605
And Facebook is in the situation where

00:12:38.605 --> 00:12:41.440
it offers everybody the platform.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.510
And I suppose, what would
you say to those people

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:46.230
who say, "He has a right
to be on Facebook"?

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:49.520
The most positive thing about social media

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.380
is that, and we forget that sometimes

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.000
when we discuss social media,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.470
is that people have been given a voice

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.540
where they otherwise
would not have a voice,

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:01.600
agency, and so many people
can actually gather,

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.030
make groups, create demonstration,

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.770
be together on social media,

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.340
where before they didn't have a voice.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.780
So, that's the most amazing
thing about social media.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:13.940
And of course, politicians
should be on social media

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:17.090
because it's also their way
of connecting with people.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.810
And they have, certainly
have a right to free speech.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:24.120
So, I don't think it was
easy for Meta to decide

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.370
whether Mr Trump should
be taken down or not.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:29.700
And we actually also touched
upon in our decision,

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.360
that Trump should not be taken down

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.390
because he was saying
falsehoods about the elections,

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:36.760
that he was not speaking the truth

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.280
about the election results.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.700
We only said that it was
right to take Mr. Trump down

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:43.990
because he was inciting violence.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:48.990
And within Facebook's own rules,
you can't incite violence.

00:13:49.243 --> 00:13:51.050
I've got the right to speak,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.820
but I don't have the right to speak,

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:56.050
if I'm actually inciting
violence against you

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:57.310
or anyone else.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.050
So that, is where we
have to draw a balance.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:04.240
And I, we thought that Meta
did that in the right place.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:08.310
But of course, Mr. Trump has
the right to be on Facebook

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:10.870
and Instagram, if he
does not incite violence.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.070
When's he coming back then?

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.680
I don't know, you have to ask Meta that.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:14.808
Is there a date?

00:14:14.808 --> 00:14:16.890
I don't know, they've given him,

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.913
I think gave him two years
or something like that.

00:14:18.913 --> 00:14:19.870
Yes.
So you have to ask Meta.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.070
He'll be back in time,

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.170
potentially for another
run at the White House.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:24.003
We'll see.
We'll see.

00:14:24.003 --> 00:14:25.095
That's up to Meta.

00:14:25.095 --> 00:14:27.450
We will see, and then you'll
look at it, of course.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.750
So that's part of the, of how
it works as we're learning.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.610
Is Facebook a platform, or a publisher?

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.390
It's a platform in my view.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:36.870
'Cause it sounds terribly
like a publisher.

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:37.703
Do you think?

00:14:37.703 --> 00:14:39.240
As an editor, in the
way you just described

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.150
that view of Donald Trump.

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:41.983
I don't think so.

00:14:41.983 --> 00:14:45.407
Because anyone who creates
a platform where people

00:14:45.407 --> 00:14:48.070
can be part of it, they
have created some rules.

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:50.940
So, I do think it's good they
have the community standards.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:51.860
They're actually really good,

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.780
the community standards that they have.

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:56.010
The problem is that they're
not always followed,

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.643
but the community standards are good, so.

00:14:58.643 --> 00:15:00.470
When you said that decision,
sorry to interrupt.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:02.490
But you said that decision
about Donald Trump,

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:04.193
just to go back to how you put it,

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.080
was not made by the community.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.960
It was actually by Meta, it wasn't by,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.600
you know, users coming forward.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:12.680
So that to me, you know, as a journalist

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.110
brings to mind an editor,

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.497
sitting in a room saying,

00:15:16.497 --> 00:15:19.890
"Well, I don't think that's
okay for us to publish".

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:20.723
Yeah, but I don't.

00:15:20.723 --> 00:15:23.420
That is a key tension that
has been about Facebook,

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:24.253
for a long time.

00:15:24.253 --> 00:15:26.890
And of course, if it admitted
to being a publisher,

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.600
it would be subject to
very different rules.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.600
Yes.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.000
So, you don't accept
that characterization?

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.880
I don't think Meta is a publisher,

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.361
I don't think they see
themselves as a publisher.

00:15:37.361 --> 00:15:38.444
But they are taking responsibility-

00:15:38.444 --> 00:15:39.740
And I would also question-
For the content.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.810
Whether they should, they
are, which they should do.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:46.480
And I think we also have to
find a new way of seeing that.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.600
That's why, there's no doubt
it would need regulation

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.013
very much like what we have...

00:15:51.013 --> 00:15:52.380
But you sound like editors.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.250
Your board, you said you
only look at content.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.400
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I can
see what your saying.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.817
Where does it tip over into,
not into being a platform

00:15:58.817 --> 00:16:00.320
and not being a publisher?

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:01.153
What keeps you....

00:16:01.153 --> 00:16:04.020
Well, I think we're well past the point

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.170
where you can just be a platform.

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.010
Because you have to have rules,

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.120
because otherwise it just goes crazy.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:11.069
You will have...

00:16:11.069 --> 00:16:11.990
You'll have to take responsibility

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:13.800
for what's being published.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:15.960
More or less, yeah you have
to take some responsib-,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.910
but doesn't mean that
doesn't make you a publisher

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.960
in the old understanding of the word.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.640
But that's, isn't that Facebook, or Meta

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:24.900
having it's cake and eating it?

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:25.750
I don't think so,

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.750
I think they are completely
entitled to have their standards

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.740
for what they want for, on their platform.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:36.340
Regulators, of course, entitled
to now regulate the area.

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.050
I think we also have to ask the question.

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:41.930
Do we want social media

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:43.740
to be a publisher?

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:44.730
No, but that's the whole point,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:46.030
a lot of people don't.
But...

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:48.090
Which is why they want Donald
Trump back on Facebook.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.830
Which I think is a fair point of view.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:51.302
But do we want-

00:16:51.302 --> 00:16:52.507
They don't want Mark Zuckerberg
as the Editor-in-Chief.

00:16:52.507 --> 00:16:55.310
But do we want social media to then be,

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:56.897
you could put anything on it.

00:16:56.897 --> 00:16:58.540
But the laws haven't kept to pace.

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:01.010
So, if you look at the law
governing this, US law,

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:03.250
this section 230, excuse me -

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:04.470
states "Social media companies,

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:05.850
shouldn't be responsible for information

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:07.270
produced by someone else."

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.060
which means a user can
publish what they want,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:11.120
and that Facebook isn't liable.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:12.020
So, which is it?

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.210
You know, you sort of Facebook
is being liable sometimes,

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:16.150
and isn't other times.

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:16.983
It's very confusing.

00:17:16.983 --> 00:17:17.816
But I think what you're asking

00:17:17.816 --> 00:17:20.040
is really underlining
that we need regulation,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:24.330
but also underlining that
you can't take social media,

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:26.800
which exists in all shapes and sizes,

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.270
in many different countries,

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:33.270
all over the world, and
squeeze it in to a regulation

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:34.760
that was for the-

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:35.593
The previous times.

00:17:35.593 --> 00:17:38.310
Yeah, previous and
other centuries outlets.

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.400
Do you come down on the side broadly,

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:41.233
taking a step back,

00:17:41.233 --> 00:17:42.510
coming away from the detail,

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:44.870
that social media is a force for good?

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.330
I do actually,

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:48.650
and I think we sometimes forget it,

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:50.500
because people are asking really,

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:51.950
really harsh questions,

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:55.780
and demanding more transparency
and criticizing Meta

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.030
and social media.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:17:58.213
And twitter and other companies of course.

00:17:58.213 --> 00:17:59.838
And fairly so.

00:17:59.838 --> 00:18:02.140
But I think we sometimes forget,

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:03.730
would we like to go back to a world

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.436
where we didn't have social media?

00:18:05.436 --> 00:18:07.870
Where we couldn't connect
with each other on,

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:09.420
on WhatsApp, for example.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:11.160
Or communicate with each other,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:12.390
get in contact with each other.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:16.170
And also, being that when I was CEO

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:17.580
of Save the Children International,

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.940
I met so many people across the world,

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:24.692
in refugee camps and women
who want to organize,

00:18:24.692 --> 00:18:29.300
labour organize, or organize
for micro-banking, or whatever.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:31.260
And they could not do these things,

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.110
without being able to
connect via social medias.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.140
Black Lives Matter,

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.190
Me Too movement, I could go on.

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:38.380
Of course they would exist,

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:41.090
and there were movements
before social media.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:42.060
But when you weigh it up,

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.000
I do think that social
media is a force for good.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.250
But, we're not there yet.

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:48.470
I wonder, are you grateful

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:51.070
you didn't grow up with social media?

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:52.840
Well, I think no,

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.180
I loved my childhood,

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.920
and I liked the simplicity of
my childhood, I really did.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.430
But I also think when you
look at young people today,

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:01.430
I have two kids.

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:04.720
We have two kids, they're 25 and 22.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.330
Honestly, I think that they
know more about the world.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:10.070
They're cleverer, they
are better than we were.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:12.800
We also have an individual responsibility

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.520
as parents to bring our children up,

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:18.000
but also children growing up to teach them

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:22.300
how to be people in the
world that we have now.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:24.650
So I don't, I think we must be very,

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:27.027
very careful not to paint this
in black and white and say,

00:19:27.027 --> 00:19:28.320
"Everything was just better".

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:29.267
I know that's not what you're doing-

00:19:29.267 --> 00:19:30.100
No, but...

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:31.830
Everything was better in the old days,

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:33.630
because there's so many good things,

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.841
particularly for people,

00:19:35.841 --> 00:19:39.040
perhaps more marginalized
people in the rest of the world

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:42.580
being heard, that has
come of social media.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.280
And I, I've met so many people who finally

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:47.767
has a voice because of social media.

00:19:47.767 --> 00:19:51.000
And we must be careful
not only to judge this by

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:55.070
the narrow lens in a Western Nordic world,

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.680
because social media is
much, much bigger than that.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:00.277
And also, I mean, just to say, you know,

00:20:00.277 --> 00:20:03.220
the societal ills that
come out on social media

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.680
were not created by social media.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:05.513
No.

00:20:05.513 --> 00:20:08.120
It's a platform to see them
in a different way as well.

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:10.350
But I was just going to ask
you about something else as we,

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.910
our time together sort of comes to an end.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.930
And it was about joylessness, and joy.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.500
Because some feel taking it
from the perspective I am,

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:19.610
at the Western perspective,
I recognize that.

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:21.700
That the internet certainly did used to be

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:22.533
a bit more fun.

00:20:22.533 --> 00:20:23.760
You know, you could share some jokes.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.640
I mean, you still can obviously.

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:25.473
Yeah.

00:20:25.473 --> 00:20:27.570
But people sort of allowed each other to,

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:30.060
to say things a bit
tongue-in-cheek, a bit sarcastic.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:31.530
And I was just looking back,

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.515
and you took a selfie when
you were Prime Minister-

00:20:34.515 --> 00:20:35.348
Yeah.

00:20:35.348 --> 00:20:36.181
You know what I'm going to be saying.

00:20:36.181 --> 00:20:37.014
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:20:37.014 --> 00:20:38.330
But for everyone else, and you were at

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:41.370
Nelson Mandela's Memorial,
you were a Prime Minister.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:43.130
And on one side was Barack Obama,

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:44.430
and on the other side was David Cameron.

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:45.770
And if I was you, I'd also be thinking,

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:46.840
this is an interesting selfie.

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:48.420
In the days before social distancing,

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.370
so you all crowd in and you have a selfie.

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:51.557
And some said, you know,

00:20:51.557 --> 00:20:54.322
"This is not what you should
be doing at a memorial".

00:20:54.322 --> 00:20:56.332
And actually you responded saying,

00:20:56.332 --> 00:20:58.159
you know, "We're people, we are people."

00:20:58.159 --> 00:20:58.992
Yeah.

00:20:58.992 --> 00:21:00.783
And it was a joyful
occasion, in many ways.

00:21:00.783 --> 00:21:03.100
It was a very joyful occasion.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:04.160
It was a memorial,

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.870
but it was at a sports stadium,

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:09.540
and the south African, like
there was drums as music,

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:10.373
there was singing.

00:21:10.373 --> 00:21:12.790
It was quite a joyous moment,

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:14.080
and almost a celebration-

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:15.650
Of his life.
Of his life, yeah.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:18.400
We sat in the corner, all the
heads, states of government,

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.950
and David Cameron sort of
muscled in where I was sitting,

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:24.250
which was close to Obama.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:25.390
We had a nice time.

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:29.360
Everyone was laughing and
I had not taken selfies

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:30.193
at this stage.

00:21:30.193 --> 00:21:31.026
This was my first selfie ever.

00:21:31.026 --> 00:21:32.519
Your first selfie-
Yeah.

00:21:32.519 --> 00:21:34.043
Was with the President of America.

00:21:34.043 --> 00:21:35.217
And I think there-
Okay.

00:21:35.217 --> 00:21:37.090
The word actually came into the dictionary

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:40.190
that year because it became so famous.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:43.570
And I just thought, "This
is where you take selfies".

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.340
So, I just learned to take
selfies from my oldest daughter.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:47.870
It's quite an impressive first selfie.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:48.703
It's quite good isn't it?

00:21:48.703 --> 00:21:49.550
Yeah,

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.620
it's not very good, I'm
the only one who looks good

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.810
in that selfie, is actually me.

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:56.940
And so, but it's actually, it was fun,

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:58.250
but people did criticize it.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:00.140
Because they thought it wasn't serious.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:01.550
And it certainly wasn't serious.

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:06.550
And it was interesting to see
that we were just three people

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:07.383
in the moment.

00:22:07.383 --> 00:22:08.910
I said, "Well it's good, to try this".

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.520
And it was like a new thing back then.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:11.840
It sounded like David Cameron really tried

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:14.330
to get in that, you were just
doing that with Barack Obama.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:15.580
It was quite funny because Michelle Obama

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:16.413
was just on the side-
Yeah.

00:22:16.413 --> 00:22:17.470
If I remember correctly of the image

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:18.610
that was taken of you taking it.

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:19.443
Yeah.

00:22:19.443 --> 00:22:21.320
Looking, you know, not in the
picture, just quite stern,

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.340
straight ahead. It was never
published, this selfie,

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:24.173
I don't think.
No, I've got it.

00:22:24.173 --> 00:22:25.108
It's on my phone.

00:22:25.108 --> 00:22:26.610
Have you framed it?

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.640
I have actually published,
because I wrote a book in,

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.340
no, I haven't framed it.

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:33.940
I've wrote a book in Denmark, in Danish.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:35.290
And it is actually in there, so you can.

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:36.270
It's in there?
It's in there.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:37.470
It's seen the light, okay-
Yeah.

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:38.457
I hadn't seen that,

00:22:38.457 --> 00:22:42.010
but I suppose just really to
get your take around that,

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:43.160
which is that, you know,

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:44.500
there's a lot of hate,

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:47.600
people don't necessarily
see politicians as people.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.790
And I just wonder, now
you're not one anymore.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:50.623
You're still married to one,

00:22:50.623 --> 00:22:53.040
but certainly you're not
a world leader anymore.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:55.020
Do you think it's at our peril

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:58.250
that we don't see our leaders as people?

00:22:58.250 --> 00:22:59.500
Yeah, I think it is.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:02.500
And I always just want people
to calm down a little bit,

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.310
because we are all people and
we have got a private life.

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:07.910
I'm not talking about
being soft on politicians,

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:10.710
and not criticizing for
them, when they do things.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.310
And I've been criticized a lot

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:14.300
and prime ministers
being criticized a lot.

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:15.940
So, that happens.

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:17.720
But maybe if we all
calm down a little bit,

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:19.080
also on social media,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.000
we would just have, we will just be nicer.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:22.930
And I think the rule should be,

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:25.340
that we try to treat
people on social media,

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:27.400
like we would talk to
each other when we met.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:30.000
And people are very polite
to each other when they meet,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.230
when they stand in the queue together,

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.200
when they take the tube, whatever.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.410
And perhaps, we should
try and take a little bit

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:37.570
of that politeness into social media,

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:40.400
and the world would
actually be a better place.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:41.260
Helle Thorning-Schmidt,
thank you very much.

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:42.357
Thank you so much for having me.

00:23:42.357 --> 00:23:44.840
And thank you so much for being with us,

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:45.880
until we meet again,

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:48.003
mask up, stay safe and good-bye.

